---

<script lang="ts">
  import DocLayout from "$lib/ui/doc/DocLayout.svelte";
  import DocHead from "$lib/ui/doc/Head.svelte";
  import DocBlock from "$lib/ui/doc/Block.svelte";
  import Code from "$lib/ui/doc/CodeBlock.svelte";
  import Example from "$lib/ui/doc/Example.svelte";
  import ListGroupConfig from "$lib/ui/doc/ConfigComponent.svelte";
  import ListItemConfig from "$lib/ui/doc/ConfigComponent.svelte";
  import { processID, editURL } from "$lib";
  let { component }: {component: any } = $props();

  import { ListGroup, ListItem } from "theui-svelte";
</script>

<DocLayout>
  <DocHead title="List Group" text="The List Group component offers a flexible, customizable way to display items like links or text in a list format. It supports various styles and types, making it ideal for structured content." edit_url={editURL("list-group")} />

  <DocBlock title="Example">
    <p class="not-prose">To use the List Group component, import it from the <code>theui-svelte</code> library. Below is a simple example demonstrating how to use the List Group:</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div>
        <ListGroup class="w-96">
          <ListItem>First Item</ListItem>
          <ListItem>Second Item</ListItem>
          <ListItem>Third Item</ListItem>
        </ListGroup>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<script>
  import { ListGroup, ListItem } from "theui-svelte";
</script>

<ListGroup>
  <ListItem>First Item</ListItem>
  <ListItem>Second Item</ListItem>
  <ListItem>Third Item</ListItem>
</ListGroup>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="List Style Variant">
    <p class="not-prose">The <code>variant</code> prop in the List Group component allows you to control the overall style of the list. It provides two options:</p>
    <ul class="my-0">
      <li><b>bordered</b>: This style adds borders around each list item, giving a defined and structured look to the list. It is useful when you want to visually separate items clearly.</li>
      <li><b>flat</b>: This style removes borders, resulting in a clean, minimalist design. It's ideal for cases where a simple and unobtrusive list presentation is preferred.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="not-prose">By default, the <code>variant</code> is set to <code>"bordered"</code>. You can easily switch between these styles to match the design needs of your application.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div>
        <ListGroup class="w-96" variant="flat">
          <ListItem>First Item</ListItem>
          <ListItem>Second Item</ListItem>
          <ListItem>Third Item</ListItem>
        </ListGroup>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<ListGroup variant="flat">
  <ListItem>First Item</ListItem>
  <ListItem>Second Item</ListItem>
  <ListItem>Third Item</ListItem>
</ListGroup>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="List Item Link">
    <p class="not-prose">The List Group always renders as an unordered list <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>, and each <code>ListItem</code> is a <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>. Add the <code>href</code> prop to a <code>ListItem</code> to render its content as a link <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> inside the <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>. Any other attributes you add to a link item, like <code>target</code>, are passed to the link. Items without <code>href</code> stay as plain list items, so you can mix static and clickable content.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div>
        <ListGroup class="w-96">
          <ListItem>Non-clickable item</ListItem>
          <ListItem href="/docs/list-group">Clickable link item</ListItem>
        </ListGroup>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<ListGroup>
  <ListItem>Non-clickable item</ListItem>
  <ListItem href="/link">Clickable link item</ListItem>
</ListGroup>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Sizes">
    <p class="not-prose">The <code>size</code> prop controls the padding of the list items. Available values are <code>"sm"</code>, <code>"md"</code> (default), <code>"lg"</code> and <code>"xl"</code>. When set on the <code>ListGroup</code>, all the items inherit it. You can also set <code>size</code> on an individual <code>ListItem</code> to override the group's size.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
        <ListGroup class="w-96" size="sm">
          <ListItem>Small item</ListItem>
          <ListItem>Small item</ListItem>
          <ListItem size="xl">Extra large item</ListItem>
        </ListGroup>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<ListGroup size="sm">
  <ListItem>Small item</ListItem>
  <ListItem>Small item</ListItem>
  <ListItem size="xl">Extra large item</ListItem>
</ListGroup>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Rounded Corner">
    <p class="not-prose">The <code>rounded</code> prop sets the border radius of the List Group. Available values are <code>"none"</code>, <code>"sm"</code>, <code>"md"</code> (default), <code>"lg"</code>, <code>"xl"</code>, <code>"2xl"</code> and <code>"full"</code>. It applies to the <code>bordered</code> variant only; a <code>flat</code> List Group has no rounded corners.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div>
        <ListGroup class="w-96" rounded="xl">
          <ListItem>First Item</ListItem>
          <ListItem>Second Item</ListItem>
          <ListItem>Third Item</ListItem>
        </ListGroup>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<ListGroup rounded="xl"> ... </ListGroup>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Animation Speed">
    <p class="not-prose">The <code>animationSpeed</code> prop controls the transition speed of the item hover effect. Available values are <code>"none"</code>, <code>"slower"</code>, <code>"slow"</code>, <code>"normal"</code> (default), <code>"fast"</code> and <code>"faster"</code>. Set it on the <code>ListGroup</code> to apply it to all items, or on a <code>ListItem</code> to override it for that item.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div>
        <ListGroup class="w-96" animationSpeed="slower">
          <ListItem>Slower hover transition</ListItem>
          <ListItem>Slower hover transition</ListItem>
          <ListItem animationSpeed="none">No transition</ListItem>
        </ListGroup>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<ListGroup animationSpeed="slower">
  <ListItem>Slower hover transition</ListItem>
  <ListItem>Slower hover transition</ListItem>
  <ListItem animationSpeed="none">No transition</ListItem>
</ListGroup>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Customization">
    <p class="not-prose">Use the <code>class</code> attribute on the <code>ListGroup</code> to style the list container. To style every item at once, use the <code>itemClasses</code> prop on the <code>ListGroup</code>. To style a single item, add the <code>class</code> attribute to that <code>ListItem</code>; it overrides <code>itemClasses</code>.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div>
        <ListGroup class="w-96 border-brand-300" itemClasses="text-brand-600 hover:bg-brand-50">
          <ListItem>First Item</ListItem>
          <ListItem class="font-bold">Second Item</ListItem>
          <ListItem>Third Item</ListItem>
        </ListGroup>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<ListGroup class="border-brand-300" itemClasses="text-brand-600 hover:bg-brand-50">
  <ListItem>First Item</ListItem>
  <ListItem class="font-bold">Second Item</ListItem>
  <ListItem>Third Item</ListItem>
</ListGroup>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
    <p class="not-prose mb-0">Anything else you pass reaches the element itself, so an <code>id</code>, a <code>title</code>, a <code>data-*</code> attribute or an event handler all work the way they would on a <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>.</p>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Accessibility">
    <div>
      <p class="not-prose">The List Group component ensures accessibility by using semantic HTML and ARIA roles:</p>
      <ol class="my-0">
        <li>The parent container <span class="not-prose"><code>&lt;ul&gt;</code></span> includes <span class="not-prose"><code>role="list"</code></span></li>
        <li>Each <span class="not-prose"><code>&lt;li&gt;</code></span> element has <span class="not-prose"><code>role="listitem"</code></span> to provide proper structure for assistive technologies.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Configuration">
    <ListGroupConfig title="List Group" component={component.listGroup} />
    <ListItemConfig title="List Item" component={component.listItem} />
  </DocBlock>

